 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Applications",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Intended for automotive powertrain and safety equipment, automotive infotainment and comfort equipment, and GHTF class A, B and C medical devices other than implants."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Dielectric",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "With the C0G (5C) dielectric, capacitance moves by 0 ±30 ppm/°C between 25 and 125 °C, and below 25 °C stays within +0.58/-0.24% at -55 °C. In the aging example the C0G line stays at 0% to beyond 1000 h while X7R falls by a few percent. High dielectric constant types (X7R, X5R, R) also lose capacitance with DC bias, vary with AC level and age."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Applied voltage",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The voltage across the part, including surges and ESD, stays at or below 100 V DC: with AC on DC the zero-to-peak value counts, and for pure AC or pulses the peak-to-peak value. Overvoltage breaks down the dielectric layers and shorts the part, after a time set by voltage and ambient temperature."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Self-heating",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "AC or pulse current heats the part through dielectric loss. The body surface, including self-heating, stays within 125 °C; for parts rated below 100 V DC the self-heating limit is 20 °C at 25 °C ambient."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Mounting stress",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Ceramic chips crack under board bending. The chip is set with its length across the direction of bending, away from board break-off lines and screw holes; slits at a break line and a router-type separator lower the stress. Cracks lower insulation resistance and can short the part, so a fuse or other fail-safe is used where a short could cause shock, smoke or fire. Parts removed from equipment or dropped are not reused."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reflow and flow soldering",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Both component and board are preheated, keeping the solder-to-component difference within ΔT≦190℃ for reflow and ΔT≦150℃ for flow on this size. Reflow below the peak recommended for the solder can bring poor wetting, voids, whiskers, weak joints, poor self-alignment and tombstoning. Flow is allowed only on 18, 21 and 31 sizes, with the fillet no higher than the chip. Repeated soldering adds up against the allowable time curves."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Adhesive and flux for flow soldering",
   "page": 29,
   "text": "An epoxy adhesive holds the chip for flow soldering; too little lets it fall off, too much fouls the land. Flux halide content is 0.1% max.; strong acid flux corrodes the part. Termination leaching is held to 25% or less of the end-face perimeter of a loose chip, and 25% or less of the edge length once mounted."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Coating and washing",
   "page": 30,
   "text": "Resin shrinkage and expansion mismatch can crack a coated chip; a low-shrinkage resin with expansion close to the chip's is chosen, a silicone undercoat buffers stress, and a low-hygroscopic resin such as epoxy keeps insulation resistance up. Ultrasonic cleaning can resonate the board and crack chips."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Environment",
   "page": 25,
   "text": "Water, brine or condensation can short the part and corrode its terminals; corrosive gases and solvents degrade its insulation over time. Sn-Zn solder lowers its reliability. The series is not safety-standard certified."
  }
 ],
